278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (Every purchase produces a bill or receipt, andeach oneisasmall record of business data. A typical receipt contains the merchant name, thedate andtime,a list of items with quantities and prices, taxes, discounts, and a final total.Individuals,small businesses, and accounting teams handle a large number of such documents,and mostofthem are still stored as paper slips or as images and PDF files. Because this information isnotheld in a structured form, it is difficult to search, compare, or verify. The BillBuster projectdeveloped during this internship converts such receipts into structured data and checks that dataautomatically. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (Errors on bills are common and are often not noticed. Aprice may be entered incorrectly, an item may be charged twice, a discount may be calculatedwrongly, or the printed total may not match the sum of the items. Checking these manually isslow and depends on the attention of the person reading the bill. An automated audit comparesthe printed total with the sum of the extracted items and highlights any difference. This givesthe user clear evidence about which receipt or item needs attention. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
